finish
/clear		! removes data from previous session (if necessary)

/prep7		! entering the preprocessing context

! Below: definition of parameters (geometric, material-related)
PANELSTIFFNESS=1E10	! this is only a starting value (in Pascals) - you are supposed to change it
WEIGHT=10		! Weight of the mass we place on the panel (in Newtons)

LX=0.5			! these geometric parameters are shown on the sketch, on the page with exercise tasks
			
DX=1.5 $ DXP=DX/2  	! the dollar sign separates two commands written in a single line
		
DY=0.3 $ DYP=DY/2
KSX=0.4			! dimensions of the chair's seat
KSY=0.4 $ KSYP=KSY/2
KSZ1=0.5		! chair's legs - height
KSZ2=0.4		! height of the back of the chair
LEGRADIUS=0.01
THICKNESSOFTHEBACK=0.01 $ GS=THICKNESSOFTHEBACK	
PANELTHICKNESS=0.05     $ GD=PANELTHICKNESS		

! a little bit of "CAD": Points (called "Keypoints" in ANSYS), Lines, Areas
K,1,LX+KSX,	KSYP,	-KSZ1
K,2,LX,		KSYP,	-KSZ1
K,3,LX,		KSYP,	0
K,4,LX+KSX,	KSYP,	0
K,5,LX+KSX,	KSYP,	KSZ2
K,6,0,		DYP,	GS/2+GD/2
K,7,DXP,	DYP,	GS/2+GD/2

/dev,font,2,,,,15	! changing the font size

K,8,LX+KSX,	-KSYP,	-KSZ1
K,9,LX,		-KSYP,	-KSZ1
K,10,LX,	-KSYP,	0
K,11,LX+KSX,	-KSYP,	0
K,12,LX+KSX,	-KSYP,	KSZ2
K,13,0,		-DYP,	GS/2+GD/2
K,14,DXP,	-DYP,	GS/2+GD/2

L,1,4 $ L,2,3 $ L,9,10 $ L,8,11
L,4,5 $ L,11,12 $ L,5,12
A,4,3,10,11
@LUKAPOWIERCH@		! define an area spanning over points 7,6, 13 and 14
			! "LUKA" means "A GAP", "something missing" in Polish

! now the board on the edge of the chair will be marked ("cut").
! which is useful to properly model the contact of the board and the chair
WPCS		! set the working plane at the origin of the coordinate system
WPOFFS,LX	! move the plane to the edge of the chair
WPROTA,0,0,90	! rotate this plane 
ASBW,all	! cut the board - at least apparently, because it will actually remain continuous

ASEL,ALL	! important! - in Ansys, each operation works only on the active set of objects
LSEL,ALL	! 
ARSYMM,X,all	! make a mirror copy of areas
LSYMM,X,all	! make a mirror copy of lines
NUMMRG,all	! sew both halves of the board (and remove any duplicate points or lines)
WPSTYLE,,,,,,,,0	! hide the workplane symbol from view

		
! execute this macro - up to this point.
! learn two methods: executing "all at once" (by entering the macro name on the command line)
! or copying piece by piece (CTRL+C fragment, CTRL+V to command line, Enter)
! watch the macro effect - understand the difference between KPLOT, LPLOT, APLOT and GPLOT
! Which display method is best here?
! if necessary, go to Help / Index - enter e.g. GPLOT and read the command description
! note that some entries have multiple occurrences in Help
! it is worth looking for "Title = Mechanical APDL Command Reference" - there the command is explained in the simplest way
! learn to rotate the model efficiently
! (how does CTRL work? does ALT work? what icon can be used to center the model in the window?)
! If you have mastered the above functions, proceed further.


! so far it was "CAD", i.e. creating the shapes of an object
! now the "CAE/FEA" will succeed, i.e. the finite element analysis: setting the appropriate material data
! then assigning boundary conditions (loads, fastenings) and finally performing calculations/viewing the results

ET,1,BEAM188		! standard beam element type
ET,2,SHELL181		! standard shell element type
ET,3,SHELL181

SECTYPE,1,BEAM,CSOLID
SECDATA,LEGRADIUS

SECTYPE,2,SHELL
SECDATA,THICKNESSOFTHEBACK	

SECTYPE,3,SHELL
@LUKAGRUBOSC@		! define here a section data corresponding to the panel thickness

@LUKAYOUNG@		! define Young's modulus typical for steel, for material data set number 1
MP,NUXY,1,0.3		


MP,EX,2,2E10		! Chair seat's stiffness; might be left as defined here
MP,NUXY,2,0.3		! Poisson's modulus - typical for most solid materials

MP,EX,3,PANELSTIFFNESS		! this is the crucial parameter in this exercise!
MP,NUXY,3,0.3


ESIZE,0.02		! setting the average size of finite elements

! note, that before any meshing, we need to select some material data and element type
ASEL,S,LOC,Z,0		! przyk³ad zawê¿ania selekcji obiektów
CM,SEAT,AREA	! the areas (or any other objects) may be conveniently grouped into "components"
MAT,2
TYPE,2
SECNUM,2
AMESH,all		! create elements on selected Areas


ASEL,S,LOC,Z,GS/2+GD/2
CM,PANEL,AREA	! creates a component out of areas forming the board/panel
MAT,3
TYPE,3
SECNUM,3
AMESH,all

CMSEL,S,SEAT
CMSEL,A,PANEL
LSLA
LSEL,INVE
MAT,1
TYPE,1
SECNUM,1
@LUKASIATKA@	! define element on all the selected lines

/ESHA,1		! show the profiles of beams and solids (it is not default!)

! does GPLOT show the correct model? a board on two chairs?
! experiment with parameters (different chair sizes, board thickness, etc.)
! then proceed to the actual calculations


/solu	! this is what the computational context is called
! first, connect the board with the chair - because the program itself will not recognize their interaction!
! You can even make materials overlap, it will not cause Error
NSEL,S,LOC,X,LX
NSEL,R,LOC,Z,0,GS/2+GD/2
CP,1,UX,all
CP,2,UY,all
CP,3,UZ,all	! tricky: linking displacements - but not rotations
! this is a reasonable approximation of the actual behavior of the board and chair
! unless we want to e.g. lift the boards (because the above CP series de facto
! ties the board to the chair, but allows it to rotate at the point of contact)
! a universal solution requires the so-called contact algorithm - a difficult matter

NSEL,S,LOC,X,-LX
NSEL,R,LOC,Z,0,GS/2+GD/2
CP,4,@LUKACP@	! do it the same way as in the case of the first chair
CP,5,@LUKACP@
CP,6,@LUKACP@


NSEL,S,LOC,Z,-KSZ1
D,all,all,0	! again a simplification - this way we permanently attach the chair legs to the floor
! here, too, the task of a contact algorithm would be ideal, but it is complicated
NSEL,ALL
NSEL,S,,,NODE(0,0,0)
F,all,FZ,-WEIGHT

ALLSEL		! do it routinely - select all before solving
@LUKAOBLICZ@		! the "heart" of simulation: perform the solution!

/post1		! entering the postprocessing context
@LUKAUGIECIE@	! show the vertical displacement (U,Z)